  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Drug-susceptible pulmonary tuberculosis, intensive phase (A15.0)</content>. Good response, with sputum smear conversion. AST 47 U/L is slightly above the upper limit, with ALT 52 and bilirubin 0.8 normal and no symptoms; this is well below the threshold for drug-induced liver injury. [S]<list><item>Continue isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide, and pyridoxine daily by video; ethambutol stays stopped.</item><item>Liver tests again at the two-month visit.</item><item>Sputum culture again at the two-month visit, which decides whether the continuation phase lasts four or seven months.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Alcohol use disorder, mild (F10.10)</content>. No alcohol for three weeks; AUDIT-C 2 for the past month. Safety reviewed: no thoughts of suicide. [S]<list><item>Continue counseling every two weeks.</item><item>Keep to no alcohol while the liver is under strain from three drugs.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Symptom review: cough, sputum, hemoptysis, fever, night sweats</td><td>At least monthly during treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Occasional dry cough only</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Weight</td><td>At least monthly during treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>61.0 kg, up 3.0 kg since diagnosis</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Hepatitis symptom review (nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, jaundice, dark urine)</td><td>Every visit and every observed dose</td><td>Completed today</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Liver tests (ALT, AST, bilirubin)</td><td>Baseline, at two weeks, then monthly because of alcohol use</td><td>Completed today</td><td>ALT 52 U/L normal, AST 47 U/L slightly high, bilirubin 0.8 mg/dL normal; no symptoms</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Visual acuity and color vision</td><td>Baseline and monthly while on ethambutol</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Ethambutol stopped a week ago, after normal checks at baseline and two weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Drug susceptibility testing of the first isolate</td><td>Once, on the first positive culture</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Susceptible to isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Sputum AFB smear and culture</td><td>Monthly until two consecutive cultures are negative</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Three smears in the third week negative; cultures from those samples pending</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Doses observed and missed (video-observed therapy)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>All doses observed</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Peripheral neuropathy check, with pyridoxine</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No neuropathy</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Home isolation and the criteria for release</td><td>Until three negative smears, clinical improvement, and two weeks of treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Release criteria met two weeks ago; back at work, with the health department&apos;s agreement</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Contact investigation (household, coworkers, patients)</td><td>Led by the health department, from diagnosis</td><td>Referred</td><td>In progress with the health department</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Alcohol use (AUDIT-C for the past month)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>AUDIT-C 2 for the past month; none in three weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Brief counseling about alcohol and liver risk</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Goal of no alcohol reinforced</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Counseling with a substance-use counselor</td><td>Every one to two weeks during treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Two sessions so far</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Medication for alcohol use disorder</td><td>Offered for alcohol use disorder</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not wanted; doing well with counseling</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide-risk review and safety plan, with the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No thoughts of suicide</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Complete 182 doses of treatment under video-observed therapy within six months. In progress; target 2026-08-18 [S]</item><item>Sputum culture negative at two months. In progress; target 2026-08-18 [S]</item><item>No alcohol during treatment, to protect his liver. In progress; target 2026-08-18 [S]</item><item>Return to work once he is no longer infectious. Outcome by 2026-03-17: Released from home isolation after three negative sputum smears and back at work four weeks into treatment [S]</item><item>Weight back to at least 64 kg by the end of treatment. In progress; target 2026-08-18 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Official American Thoracic Society/Centers for Disease Control and Prevention/Infectious Diseases Society of America Clinical Practice Guidelines: Treatment of Drug-Susceptible Tuberculosis (2016); An official ATS statement: hepatotoxicity of antituberculosis therapy (2006); Guidelines for the investigation of contacts of persons with infectious tuberculosis: recommendations from the National Tuberculosis Controllers Association and CDC (2005); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Substance Use Disorders (2021); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Two-month review with sputum culture and liver tests. Next scheduled visit 2026-04-14: end of the intensive phase: two-month sputum and the start of the continuation phase. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
